    <header>
      <div class="tag">project 01 · reverse engineering</div>
      <h1>Game Internals &amp; Anti-Cheat Research</h1>
      <div class="meta"><span>C++</span><span>x86</span><span>memory analysis</span><span>DLL injection</span><span>hooking</span></div>
    </header>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>A reverse-engineering study of a game engine's runtime memory model, done to
      understand how cheat techniques work mechanically and — more importantly — how
      anti-cheat systems detect them. The goal was learning low-level Windows internals
      and RE methodology, not gaining an advantage in live play.</p>

    <h2>What I built / figured out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Memory model mapping:</strong> located the entity list and resolved multi-level pointer chains to read player state reliably across updates.</li>
      <li><strong>World-to-screen:</strong> reimplemented the engine's view-matrix math to project 3D positions to 2D screen space (the basis of an ESP overlay).</li>
      <li><strong>Injection &amp; hooking:</strong> used DLL injection and function hooking to intercept and observe engine calls at runtime.</li>
      <li><strong>Detection surface:</strong> documented what each technique exposes to an anti-cheat — handle access patterns, injected modules, hook signatures — and how detection vs. evasion plays out.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Demo (proof of concept)</h2>
    <p>Short recorded demo on a private/offline setup, showing the technique rather than gameplay:</p>
    <!-- EMBED: upload clip to YouTube as UNLISTED, paste embed URL. No video files in the repo. -->
    <div class="video"><iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID" title="Proof of concept" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
