    <h2>Selected technique (snippet)</h2>
    <p>An illustrative slice of the world-to-screen projection — included to show the reasoning, not as runnable cheat software:</p>
    <pre>// view-matrix projection: world (x,y,z) -> screen (sx,sy)
bool WorldToScreen(const Vec3&amp; pos, const float m[16], Vec2&amp; out) {
    float w = m[12]*pos.x + m[13]*pos.y + m[14]*pos.z + m[15];
    if (w &lt; 0.01f) return false;            // behind the camera
    float x = m[0]*pos.x + m[1]*pos.y + m[2]*pos.z + m[3];
    float y = m[4]*pos.x + m[5]*pos.y + m[6]*pos.z + m[7];
    out.x = (screenW * 0.5f) * (1.f + x / w);
    out.y = (screenH * 0.5f) * (1.f - y / w);
    return true;
}</pre>

    <h2>How it maps to security work</h2>
    <p>These are the same primitives used in <strong>malware analysis</strong> and
      <strong>red teaming</strong>: reading and writing another process's memory,
      resolving pointers in a live address space, injecting code, hooking API calls,
      and reasoning about what a defensive product (here anti-cheat; in industry, EDR)
      can observe and how evasion is detected.</p>

    <div class="note"><p><strong>Context &amp; ethics.</strong> Conducted on my own systems
      against offline/local targets to learn reverse engineering and Windows internals.
      No software was deployed against live players, and no runnable cheat is distributed
      here — this page documents technique and analysis only.</p></div>
